@node Enabling the mode
@section Enabling the mode

@findex org-table-wrap-mode
@findex global-org-table-wrap-mode

The command @code{org-table-wrap-mode} is a buffer-local minor mode that activates visual word-wrapping for Org tables in the current buffer.  When enabled, all tables in the buffer are scanned: those wider than the window receive an overlay that displays the wrapped rendering.  The mode lighter is @samp{OTW}. @*

Invoke it interactively with @samp{M-x org-table-wrap-mode}, or enable it from your init file for all Org buffers: @*

@lisp
(add-hook 'org-mode-hook #'org-table-wrap-mode)
@end lisp

While the mode is active, a @code{post-command-hook} tracks point movement.  When point enters a wrapped table, the overlay is removed so you can edit the underlying Org table normally.  When point leaves, the overlay is re-applied.  This reveal-on-enter behavior is similar to what @samp{org-appear} does for emphasis markers and links. @*

A @code{window-size-change-functions} hook ensures that all wrapped tables are re-processed when the window is resized.  Resize events are debounced with a 0.2-second idle timer to avoid redundant work during rapid resizing (such as dragging a window edge). @*

If the buffer is not yet displayed in any window when the mode is enabled (for example, when a file is opened in the background), processing is deferred via @code{window-configuration-change-hook} until the buffer becomes visible.  This avoids errors from trying to measure pixel widths without a live window. @*

When the mode is disabled, all overlays are removed, the invisibility spec is cleaned up, and the hooks are removed.  The global resize hook is only removed when no other buffer in the session uses @code{org-table-wrap-mode}. @*

The command @code{global-org-table-wrap-mode} is a globalized minor mode that automatically enables @code{org-table-wrap-mode} in every Org buffer.  This is the most convenient way to use the package: @*

@lisp
(global-org-table-wrap-mode 1)
@end lisp

